==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/stɛl/}}
* {{rhyme|en|ɛl|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*stel-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|stellen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|stellan|t=to give a place to, set, place}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*stalljan|t=to put, position}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*stel-|t=to place, put, post, stand}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|stellen|t=to set, put}}, dated {{cog|nds|stellen|t=to put, place, fix}}, {{cog|de|stellen|t=to set, place, provide}}, {{cog|ang|steall|t=position, place}}. More at {{l|en|stall}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|+|+|+,stold}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|UK|_|dialectal|Scotland}} To place in [[position]]; [[set up]], fix, [[plant]]; [[prop]], [[mount]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1902|author=John Buchan|title=The Outgoing of the Tide
|passage=How he escaped a broken neck in that dreadful place no human being will ever ken. The sweat, he has told me, stood in cold drops upon his forehead; he scarcely was aware of the saddle in which he sat, and his eyes were '''stelled''' in his head so that he saw nothing but the sky [[ayont]] him.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[portray]]; [[delineate]]; [[display]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Lucrece|line=1443–44|text=To this well-painted piece is Lucrece come,<br>To find a face where all distress is '''stelled'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|24|text=Mine eye hath play'd the painter and hath '''stell'd'''<br>Thy beauty's form in table of my heart {{...}}}}

===Etymology 2===
Alteration of {{m|en|stall}}, after the verb {{m|en||to stell}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[place]]; [[station]].
# A [[stall]]; a [[fold]] for cattle.
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} A [[prop]]; a [[support]], as for the feet in standing or climbing.
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} A [[still]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1786|author=w:Robert Burns|title=The Author's Earnest Cry And Prayer
|passage=Paint Scotland greetin owre her thrissle;<br>Her mutchkin stowp as toom's a whissle;<br>An' damn'd excisemen in a bussle,<br>Seizin a '''stell''',<br>Triumphant crushin't like a mussel,<br>Or limpet shell!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1791|author=w:Robert Burns|title=Such a Parcel of Rogues in a Nation
|passage=The English '''stell''' we could disdain,<br>Secure in valour's station;<br>But English gold has been our bane—<br>Such a parcel of rogues in a nation!}}

